#ifndef VERSION_MBM_H
#define VERSION_MBM_H

#ifndef MBM_VERSION
/*
    Format: "X.Y" or "X.Y.Z"
    X - Major change
    Y - New functionalities
    Z - Fix some issues or even improved non impact of new functionalities (optional patch component)

    Historical entries below predate the "Z" patch component and used a trailing
    letter (e.g. "3.1.a") instead; new entries should prefer numeric "X.Y.Z".

    1.0.a Framework began using Directx
    1.2.a Framework changed to Opengl-ES
    1.3.a Added wrapper Java for Android
    1.9.e Removed Directx
    2.0.a Added LUA as Language script
    2.1.a Added Editor LUA (sprite maker, scene2dw,scene2ds,scene3d)
    2.2.a Added Editor LUA (particle editor)
    2.3.a Added Editor LUA (font editor)
    2.4.a Changed internal way of binary mesh (introduced version as define)
    2.5.x Added TMX binary file
    2.6.x General re work
    2.7.x Included CMake
    2.8.a Make shared library of core-mbm
    3.0.a Plugins introduced (ImGui, box2d) as Modules
    3.1.a Sprite maker editor based on ImGui plugin added
    3.1.b Improved / fix Sprite maker editor based on ImGui
    3.1.c Created Shader editor based on ImGui / fix Minor issue on Shader Lua
    3.1.d Created Scene 2d Editor / removed old scenes editor and resources
    3.1.e Scene 2d Editor added isRelative2ds option
    3.1.f Updated sqlite3 to version 3.24.0. (fixed issue: ERROR attempt to index a string value) replaced luaL_register by luaL_newlib.
    3.2.a Added Tile Map from own engine. Removed TMX support
    3.2.b Added texture packer editor
    3.2.c Fixed endFX issue
    3.2.e Update animation even when is not in the screen
    3.2.f Fixed issue for vertex shader (when set to null it was not freeing )
    3.2.g Added compatibility to load Triangle from version 3
    3.2.f Parser for txt on font created
    3.2.g Upgrade IM-GUI version to 1.78, tinyfiledialog (v3.6.3)
    3.3   mini-mbm on windows does not depend anymore on core_mbm
    3.4   refactoried files on LUA wrapper
    3.5   Frozen box 2d (2.3.2)
    3.6   Migrated to box2d (2.4.1)
    4.0   LiquidFun for box 2d, this version forces to drawback Box2D to 2.3.0.
    4.1   LiquidFun for box 2d 2.3.0 and box 2d 2.4.1 in library separated
    4.2   Reorganized framework, moved needed function to plugins, LiquidFun and Box2d Available.
    5.0   Include Directx 9 backend support. Refactoried core-manager-opengl_es to core-manager-renderer with support to Directx9 and Opengl-ES and future renderers.
    5.1   Updated IM-GUI version to 1.92.6
    5.2   Introduced MacOs support
    5.3   Modernized Android Project, Added native Activity,removed old Java wrapper for AUDIO jni, added support to generate project on Android Studio using cmake
    6.0   The first Mini MBM lighting implementation using the classic model of Phong.
    6.1   TextureAnimationEffect ownership normalized to animation-level metadata with explicit legacy mismatch validation.
    6.2   Mesh v10 stores TextureAnimationEffect once per animation FX block instead of duplicating it in PS/VS stage records.
    6.3   Mesh v11 stores TextureAnimationEffect once per animation FX block instead of duplicating it in PS/VS stage records.
    6.4   Lighting system created with support multiple light sources and improved performance. Mesh v12 introduces a new lighting.
    6.5.0 Added reusable Blender-style 3D orbit navigation gizmo (editor_utils.lua drawOrbitGizmo) to mesh_debug's Camera window; split the Light panel into its own window. First entry using the X.Y.Z format.
    6.6.0 Added Scene Editor 3D (editor/scene_editor3d.lua): grid/free placement of any renderizable, per-layer Y control, cached mesh thumbnails via render2texture, async mesh loading with progress, and a self-contained requirable scene export.
    6.6.1 Fixed Scene Editor 3D: Combo() off-by-one index bug (Map type/snap-scale combos), ColorEdit4 crash in the light panel (wrong calling convention + wrong 0-255 color range), background color menu parity with Scene Editor 2D, thumbnail cache location, per-tab orbit camera, sync reload of already-cached meshes, and scroll-into-scene leak while hovering ImGui windows.
    6.6.2 Added tImGui.GetWantCaptureMouse()/GetWantCaptureKeyboard() (plugins/imGui): expose io.WantCaptureMouse/WantCaptureKeyboard for reading, the correct/documented way to decide whether input should reach the game scene vs the UI, replacing IsAnyWindowHovered() which repeatedly let clicks/scroll on Scene Editor 3D's own windows leak into the 3D scene. Also fixed the editor's stray white render-to-texture quad, mislabeled/too-short camera zoom, and made Layer-tab placement synchronous.
    6.7.0 Physic Editor: fixed a crash loading 3D meshes (selection/highlight API was 2D-only); added a real 3D orbit camera + nav gizmo, mouse-based selection of physics shapes via native :collide(), and full editing support for all physics types on 3D meshes. Added a "Complex" primitive (8-point box, plus a 12-triangle decomposition) to the Add-Physic UI. Mesh Debug: added a bulk "Reset Physics To" operation in Apply-to-all, computing each mesh's own frame-1 vertex bounds instead of assuming mesh:getSize().
    6.8.0 Added a real ray/AABB test for 3D screen-space picking: DEVICE::rayIntersectsAABB (slab method) plus a new mbm.getPickRay(sx,sy) Lua binding exposing DEVICE::rayCast (origin + normalized direction), previously only used internally to back mbm.to3d. Fixed obj:collide(x,y) on 3D objects to use this instead of unprojecting at a guessed depth (the object's own raw world Z, treated as camera distance) -- that approximation degenerated to the camera's own position for any object near world Z=0 (e.g. anything at its default placement position), so the test could never register a hit there; it also mattered generally for any free-orbiting camera since an object's Z has no fixed relationship to its real camera distance. Also fixed obj:collide(x, y, useAABB) (the 4-argument form): onCheckCollisionBoundingBoxRenderizable only accepted an argument count of exactly 3, so passing the documented optional 4th useAABB argument missed that branch entirely and hit a generic "Expected: [renderizable],[renderizable] or [x,y]" error instead. Discovered and fixed while building Scene Editor 3D's Shift+drag rectangle-select. Added Scene Editor 3D: grid show/hide toggle, Free-mode grid bordering (snapping still skipped), live mesh-offset propagation to preview/placed instances, Main Scene/Mesh property tab renames, replace-not-duplicate placement on occupied grid cells, and a full select/rotate/delete workflow (multi-select, Shift+drag rectangle select, Esc to unselect, bottom-right rotate/delete tool window) mirroring tilemap_editor.lua.
    6.9.0 Added a true AABB-center concept, engine-wide: RENDERIZABLE::getBoundingAABBCenterOffset() (new, Impl-backed, virtual) reports the world-space offset from an object's pivot to its actual geometric center -- (0,0,0) when geometry is centered on its own pivot (the common case), non-zero for anything anchored elsewhere (a mesh pivoted at its floor/base, a character at its feet). Backed by a new INFO_PHYSICS::getBoundsMinMax() (both 2D/3D), which also properly unions bounds across every physics shape kind on a mesh, unlike getBounds(). New Lua binding obj:getAABBCenter(). TEXT_DRAW overrides the offset directly from its own alignment-driven aabbMin/aabbMax. Replaced the old doOffsetIfText()/undoOffsetIfText() mutate-position-then-restore hack (font-only, used inside obj:collide) and editor_utils.lua's independently-written font-only correction in setShapeToMesh with this one general mechanism -- obj:collide and the shared 2D editor selection-highlight shape now both use it uniformly. Also fixed INFO_PHYSICS::getBounds()'s CUBE/SPHERE branches (both 2D/3D overloads), which derived size via 2*max(|absCenter-halfDim|,|absCenter+halfDim|) -- only correct when absCenter==(0,0,0); for an off-center shape (e.g. the single CUBE MESH_MBM_DEBUG::fillAtLeastOneBound() auto-bakes into any mesh saved with no hand-authored physics, whose absCenter is the mesh's own true center) this could inflate the reported size up to 2x on the anchored axis. Physic Editor: new physics shapes now default their position to the mesh's true AABB center instead of the hardcoded local origin, and a small marker (distinct from the existing hover-highlight point) shows that center while editing. Scene Editor 3D: hover wire-box and Mesh Set thumbnail camera focus now use the true center instead of the pivot. Root-caused while investigating a Scene Editor 3D hover-box bug reported against a floor-anchored test building mesh.
    6.10.0 Scene Editor 3D (Map edition tab): replaced the plain yellow wireframe hover box with a solid translucent shape (green when the hovered/selected mesh belongs to the active layer, red otherwise; selected meshes blink between alpha 0.2-0.5, a per-frame sine wave, no shader needed), mirroring tilemap_editor.lua's/physic_editor.lua's own tinted-overlay visual language instead of a bespoke wireframe. Added Ctrl+Click to toggle one mesh into/out of the current selection (additive), complementing the existing Shift+drag rectangle-select (which replaces). Placed Meshes list rows and the active layer's "(active)" label now render in green to match. Mesh property tab: added per-asset Rotation and Scale offsets (added on top of each instance's own rotation/scale, same as the existing Position offset), applied consistently in the live preview, every placed instance, and the exported game script's runtime loader. Map tab: directional light direction is now edited via the same orbit trackball widget already used for the camera (tUtil.drawOrbitGizmo, reused unmodified since it only touches .azimuth/.elevation) instead of three raw x/y/z InputFloats, plus a visual arrow in the 3D scene showing where the light comes from and a small tinted marker at each point light's position -- there was previously no way to see where a light actually was/pointed relative to the placed scene, only numbers in a panel.
    6.10.1 Fixed MESH_MBM_DEBUG::getInfo(filePath, ...) (the lightweight file-only info query used by Mesh Debug's UI, e.g. the Normals/Light 3D panels) never reading each mesh's actual hasNormal/hasUv flags from its SECTION_FRAME_STATIC payload -- it only counted frames, leaving hasNorText at its struct-default (normals always false), so freshly-saved normals appeared to vanish from the UI even though MESH_MBM_DEBUG::loadV11 (the real load path used for rendering) already parsed them correctly and the file was fine on disk. Now parses frame 0's FRAME_HEADER_V11 the same way loadV11 does.
    6.11.0 Fixed two reserved-lighting-shader bugs affecting every 3D lit mesh, in all three render backends (OpenGL ES, DirectX9, Metal): (1) Directional light appeared to change with camera orbit. Root cause: the per-object matrix uploaded as the vertex shader's "mvMatrix" uniform (which builds vNormalView/vPositionView) was SHADER::modelView -- the model matrix only, never multiplied by the camera's view matrix -- so despite the name, those varyings were actually world-space, not view-space, while getLightDirectionView()/getLightPositionView()/getPointLightPositionView() already correctly rotated (and, for position, translated) light data into true view space via camera.matrixView/matrixView2d. Dotting a world-space normal against a view-space light direction is what made lighting swing with camera orbit. Fixed by adding a second static, SHADER::mvMatrixLightSpace (modelView x camera view, computed by the new SHADER::updateMvpAndLightMatrices() helper alongside the existing mvpMatrix), and uploading that instead of modelView for "mvMatrix" -- SHADER::modelView itself, and the mvpMatrix path, are untouched. This also fixes a latent, previously-unreported 2D bug: point-light attenuation was already silently wrong any time a 2D world camera panned away from the origin, for the same world/view-space mismatch reason. (2) Point lights had no visible effect on 3D meshes. Root cause: getReservedLightMode() hardcoded 3D to directional-only (mode 1) or off (mode 0) -- the point-light per-pixel array path (mode 2) was only ever selected for 2D world sprites, even though mbm.addPointLight('3d', ...) correctly stored the light and DEVICE's per-object point-light selection was already fully LIGHT_TARGET-generic. Fixed by making 3D's mode 1 run the directional term AND a point-light loop together (a new DirectionalColor uniform keeps the two from clobbering the same LightColor[0] slot), instead of introducing a third mode -- so a 3D mesh can now be lit by its directional light and any nearby point lights at once, matching how a real scene's lighting composes. 2D's existing point-only behavior (mode 2) is untouched.
    6.12.0 Mesh Debug: the Normals tree node now visualizes frame-1's per-vertex normals as lines whenever it's open (any type with normals -- sprite/tile/mesh/etc, not just 'mesh' -- drawn in 2dw or 3d space to match the current camera mode), color-coded green/red by comparing each stored normal against the geometric face normal derived from triangle winding (meshD:getIndex/getVertex), so a wrong-facing normal is obvious without guessing. The geometric-normal computation accounts for meshD:getModeFrontFace() (mini-mbm's own mesh default is CW, not the textbook CCW-is-outward convention), otherwise every correctly-wound mesh would be reported as universally flipped -- an earlier iteration of this also called the C++-internal method name (getModeFrontFaceDirection) instead of the actual Lua-bound one (getModeFrontFace), which just no-ops via this file's pcall wrapper and silently left every normal miscategorized. Added a per-subset vertex table (nx/ny/nz DragFloats, OK/Flipped status, per-row and per-subset-bulk Flip and Recompute-from-geometry actions, kept to 5 narrow columns plus a table-local horizontal scrollbar so it fits the Mesh Tree window's default width) so individual normals can be fixed inline instead of only globally via Add/Remove Normals. Correctness coloring and Recompute are skipped (with a note) when the mesh's draw mode isn't TRIANGLES, since face winding isn't well-defined there; Flip still works. Also fixed the pre-existing Camera/Light 3D panels (showCameraWindow/showLightWindow) rendering off the right edge of the screen on macOS: their right-anchored SetNextWindowPos math used mbm.getSizeScreen() (logical/scaled points) instead of mbm.getRealSizeScreen() (physical framebuffer pixels, the space SetNextWindowPos actually operates in), which only diverges on HiDPI/Retina displays. Fixed a normal-viz line leak: showMeshTreeWindow's per-mesh tree node only calls showMeshOptions (which owned the Normals node's auto-cancel-on-close) for whichever entry is currently expanded/selected, so switching to a different loaded mesh left the previous entry's normal lines destroy-less and permanently visible, stacking up across every mesh visited; the outer loop's collapsed-row branch now destroys them unconditionally instead of relying on showMeshOptions running. Applied the same fix to the pre-existing, identically-shaped leak in the Transform tab's preview clone (tXformPreviewMesh). Purely an editor/ *.lua addition/fix, no engine/Lua-binding changes.
    6.13.0 Two follow-up fixes to 6.11.0's lighting round, both found by testing real scenes (game-template/scenes/light-multi-test.lua and a floor mesh in Scene Editor 3D) rather than by inspection: (1) 2D point lights on a sprite with no normal map (HasNormalMap == 0) regressed from a soft glow to a tiny, sharp, geometry-free hotspot. Root cause: that path still ran the real diffuse dot-product (max(dot(normalView, lightDir), 0)) against a synthetic flat normalView=(0,0,1), and 6.11.0's mvMatrix fix removed an old, accidental, asymmetric Z-offset between light and fragment position that used to make that dot product forgiving almost everywhere -- once symmetric (correct), the true (tiny) Z-gap between an in-plane light and an in-plane sprite collapsed the dot product to a narrow hotspot. Fixed by skipping the angle term entirely for this case (diffuse = 1.0 when HasNormalMap == 0) -- distance-only falloff, matching how 2D point lights work in most other engines. Sprites with a real bound normal map (HasNormalMap != 0, e.g. src/test-lib/box.spt + wooden-box_normal.png) are unaffected: they keep the real per-pixel dot product, which is supposed to be sharp/angle-sensitive since that's the entire point of a normal map. (2) Any 3D mesh with lighting enabled and zero point lights configured was silently picking up a phantom point light. Root cause: selectPointLightsForObjectImpl()'s pre-existing fallback to LIGHT_STATE's single legacy point-light slot (for games that only ever called mbm.setPointLight, never addPointLight) fired whenever the point-light list was empty, with no check for whether that legacy slot had ever actually been configured -- so it silently exposed LIGHT_STATE's bare struct defaults (pointPosition=(0,0,128), radius=512, white) as a fake selected light. Harmless while 3D never consumed point-light selection at all; very much not once 6.11.0 made it start to. Fixed by gating that fallback on lightState.pointPositionConfigured/pointColorConfigured/pointRadiusConfigured (added specifically for this purpose) -- the fallback now only fires for targets where a legacy setter was actually called at least once. docs/light.md gained a beginner-level "Quick Mental Model" section (the three lighting terms, where the Normal comes from in each of the three common cases, the 2D light-height/Z convention, and a diagnostic checklist) since both of these were found by a user working through exactly that reasoning by hand.
    6.14.0 Two MESH_MBM_DEBUG bugs found while building a "known-good" reference mesh to test normals/lighting against (src/core_mbm/mesh-manager.cpp): (1) calculateNormals() (the auto-recompute-from-geometry path behind meshDebug:addNormals()) used the wrong SUBSET_DEBUG fields for its indexed-mesh loop bound (vertexStart/vertexCount instead of indexStart/indexCount) and had an off-by-one loop condition (strict '<' instead of '<=') in both the indexed and non-indexed branches -- together these meant auto-normal generation silently produced all-zero normals for the overwhelmingly common case (vertex count != index count), and always skipped each subset's last triangle even when it did run. (2) addIndex() -- the from-scratch mesh-building API used by meshDebug:addIndex() -- applied its vertexStart offset correction to the wrong slice of the index buffer when appending a new subset's indices (it read the not-yet-updated pSubset->indexStart instead of the already-correct indexCountBefore computed earlier in the same call), silently corrupting every previously-added subset's stored indices each time a further subset's indices were added; a related wrong-source-offset bug in the same function's index-buffer memmove (copying "after" indices from oldIndex[0] instead of oldIndex[indexCountBefore+oldSizeIndex]) was fixed alongside it. Both bugs are latent in the from-scratch mesh-authoring path used by any tool that builds a multi-subset mesh one subset at a time (the ordinary, documented way) -- verified via a controlled reproduction (4-vertex/6-index quad, then a 24-vertex/6-subset cube) that came back with garbage/zeroed indices and normals before the fix and correct ones after. Mesh Debug: added an "Add Colored Cube" menu item (right below "Load from folder") that builds a 24-vertex, 6-subset reference cube in Lua via the same from-scratch meshDebug API, one solid hex-texture color per face (no shared vertices, so addNormals() can't smooth-average across faces), saves it to src/test-lib/colored_cube.msh, and loads it into the tree like any other mesh -- a repeatable, trustworthy reference/orientation asset now that the two bugs above no longer stand in the way. Two more issues surfaced by actually testing the generated cube: (3) MESH::render() (src/render/mesh.cpp) draws nothing at all for a mesh with zero animations, even a static single-frame one, since it looks up the current animation before doing any drawing and bails out if there isn't one -- the cube builder now calls meshDebug:addAnim() to add a trivial frame-1-to-1 animation so the cube (and any similarly-built from-scratch mesh) renders immediately. (4) The cube visually rendered inside-out (its true outward faces culled away, showing the inside instead) with setModeFrontFace('CCW'), despite CCW being the mathematically correct description of its winding under calculateNormals()'s uncorrected cross product -- this engine's actual screen-space rasterization treats that same winding as CW-front, matching the already-documented fact that mini-mbm's own mesh convention is CW, not the textbook CCW-is-outward one. Fixed by setting 'CW' instead; normals and culling are independent mechanisms here so this doesn't affect the (separately verified) per-face normals. Also replaced Mesh Debug's raw X/Y/Z directional-light sliders with the same orbit-trackball gizmo already used for the camera and for Scene Editor 3D's own light direction; the azimuth/elevation<->direction-vector conversion (previously duplicated in scene_editor3d.lua) is now shared via editor_utils.lua's tUtil.dirFromOrbit/tUtil.orbitFromDir. Also fixed mesh_debug.lua's Light panel (getPreviewStage2Texture) and Shader tree node spamming "[mesh_debug]... type of class [1][mesh] not implemented for FX!!!" to the console every frame whenever the previewed mesh has no active shader animation (e.g. any static mesh like this new cube) -- MESH::getFx() legitimately returns null in that case, an expected/frequent state, not a bug worth logging on every single frame; switched from the print-on-failure dpCall wrapper to a plain pcall for that specific probe.
    6.15.0 Fixed a real false-positive/false-negative bug in Mesh Debug's Normals tree node OK/Flipped check (editor/mesh_debug.lua, computeGeoNormalsForSubset), found while cross-checking the new colored cube (6.14.0) against base.msh. The check compared each stored normal against a geometric cross(p1-p0, p2-p0) that it sign-flipped whenever the mesh's declared front-face wasn't CCW, on the assumption that CW-declared meshes have their raw cross product pointing inward -- but that assumption doesn't hold in general: culling (glFrontFace, screen-space triangle winding) and lighting (the stored per-vertex normal) are independent mechanisms with no fixed relationship, and whether a mesh's raw cross product happens to point outward or inward depends on how its vertices were actually wound, not on its front-face string. Confirmed both ways: the hand-built colored cube (front_face=CW, raw cross genuinely outward, verified correct by actual rendering) was misreported as universally "Flipped"; base.msh (a Blender export, also front_face=CW) was misreported as universally "OK" despite its top and bottom faces having genuinely swapped normals -- confirmed independently by classifying vertices via actual Y position rather than trusting their stored normal, and by a live light test (an overhead light lit the bottom face instead of the top). The old heuristic wasn't just imprecise, it was mathematically guaranteed to rubber-stamp that exact swapped-normal defect as correct. Fixed by always using the same unconditional raw cross product MESH_MBM_DEBUG::calculateNormals() itself uses (no front-face sign flip), so the check answers its one real question -- does the stored normal agree with what this engine's own "Recompute from geometry" would produce -- consistently. User-verified after the fix: recomputing base.msh's normals now correctly resolves its swapped top/bottom (status OK, lit from the expected side), and the colored cube's normals read OK as expected. Also added a read-only X/Y/Z direction readout (one line per axis, to avoid overflowing the panel width) below the directional-light orbit gizmo in both Mesh Debug and Scene Editor 3D, so the gizmo's current direction is visible as numbers, not just a trackball position.
    6.16.0 BREAKING: COLOR::getColorFromHexString/getStringHexColorFromColor (include/core_mbm/primitives.h) switched from the engine's historical alpha-first "#AARRGGBB" hex string convention to the standard alpha-last "#RRGGBB"/"#RRGGBBAA" convention (matches CSS and the VS Code color-swatch decorator, and the bundled ImGui color picker already used this order internally). This is the format used by TEXTURE::load's "#..." solid-color convenience path, obj:setTexture(r,g,b,a), and Tiled color-typed custom properties -- plain obj:setColor(r,g,b,a) float calls are unaffected. TEXTURE::loadSolidColor (src/core_mbm/texture-manager.cpp) now calls the shared COLOR::getColorFromHexString instead of reimplementing alpha-first parsing independently. Updated every hardcoded hex literal in the tree to the new order: editor/mesh_debug.lua's colored-cube face colors, editor/scene_editor2d.lua's selection-overlay colors and hand-built hex format string, plugins/tiled/tile_editor.cpp's brick placeholder colors, plugins/box2d-liquid-fun-lua's default particle texture, and src/core_mbm/texture-manager.cpp's normal/emissive fallback textures. KNOWN COMPATIBILITY GAP: any .msh file saved before this version with a Tiled BTILE_PROPERTY_TYPE_COLOR custom property stores that color as an old-format alpha-first hex string inside the binary tile-property data; since both conventions are indistinguishable 8-hex-digit strings there is no reliable auto-detection, so such saved properties will decode with shifted channels after upgrading and must be re-entered/re-saved in the Tiled/tile map editor.
    6.17.0 Scene Editor 3D: added a real Save/Export split, mirroring Scene Editor 2D's existing convention (editor/scene_editor3d.lua). Save (Ctrl+S / Save As, now enforcing a .scene3d-edit.lua extension, reopened via Load) is unchanged in spirit; a new "Export Game Scene..." menu item (writeScene3d/getSceneLoaderCode3d, now parameterized by an async/sync mesh-loading choice instead of forked into a second writer, since every field the generator already wrote -- camera, tLightConfig, tMeshOffsets, tSceneObjects, tPlacedMeshInfo -- turned out to already be real gameplay data with nothing editor-only left to strip) writes a `<name>-scene3d.lua` a developer can `require` directly into a real game, with Play now exercising the same generated code path. New developer-facing accessors on the generated tScene3d: getObject(name) (alongside the existing dual-mode get(indexOrName), kept as-is per explicit request rather than merged), getAmbientLight(), getDirectionalLight(), getPointLight(index-or-none-for-all), and getSceneObject(name)/getAllSceneObjects() for the Map tab's marker objects (spawn points/paths/triggers), which previously had no accessor at all despite being written into every export. A new checkbox lets the developer choose loadAsync vs synchronous load for 'mesh'-type placed objects specifically (the only render type with a real async loader in this engine) -- baked directly into the generated file's code text at export/play time (only one of the two branches is ever emitted), not a runtime flag. Three bugs found and fixed along the way, all of which would otherwise ship into every exported scene: (1) tScene3d.addMesh inferred an object's render type via the editor-only `meshDebug` global (from mesh_debug.lua's Mesh Set tool), which is always nil in a shipped game -- silently always falling back to type 'mesh' regardless of the actual file; replaced with a self-contained file-extension lookup table with no editor dependency. (2) The generated tScene3d.load's point-light replay never called mbm.clearPointLights('3d') before its addPointLight loop (unlike the live editor's own applyLightConfigToEngine, which does) -- harmless for a single load, but would accumulate stale point lights across repeated calls. (3) Point light alpha defaulted to `pl.a or 255` in two places (the live editor and the generated loader) despite colors being 0.0-1.0 floats everywhere else in this file -- corrected to `pl.a or 1`. All verified headlessly: a scratch scene drove the real writeScene3d/getSceneLoaderCode3d functions with mock state, producing real exported files for both the async and sync choice, confirming (via static grep) each file contains only its own branch's code and the two light-replay fixes, then requiring each file back in, calling :load(), and exercising every accessor end-to-end.
    6.18.0 Fixed two real bugs in Scene Editor 3D's scene reload (onOpenScene3d, editor/scene_editor3d.lua), found from a user report of a reload stuck at "400 / 800" after saving a scene with 400 placed meshes. (1) addPlacedMesh's async branch unconditionally called beginLoadProgress(tLoadProgress.iTotal + 1, ...) on every single placement, both growing the total by 1 per call AND resetting the loaded counter back to 0 -- onOpenScene3d already sets the correct total once, up front, before its reload loop, so each of its 400 addPlacedMesh calls re-stomped that total (400 -> 800) while repeatedly zeroing progress, so the modal could never reach 100%. Fixed by only (re)starting the tracker when no bulk load is already in progress, leaving a caller's own pre-set total alone. (2) Investigating further (why only one mesh ended up visible despite the placed-mesh count being correct) reproduced a genuine crash, not just a stuck modal: firing hundreds of concurrent mesh:loadAsync() calls for the same file back-to-back segfaults inside the Lua registry lookup in the async load callback (mesh-lua.cpp's onLoadAsyncMeshLua, called from MESH_MANAGER::pumpAsyncLoads) -- confirmed via gdb backtrace. This is a real bug in the async mesh-loading pipeline under heavy same-file concurrency, out of scope to redesign here since it's shared infrastructure beyond this one editor. A scene reload has no actual need for non-blocking loads in the first place, so onOpenScene3d's reload loop now passes bSync=true (synchronous placement) instead, sidestepping the crash entirely; addPlacedMesh's synchronous branch now also increments the progress counter so the modal still reports real progress. Verified headlessly with a 400-mock-mesh reload: no crash, progress reaches 400/400, and every placed mesh ends up with a real loaded object. The underlying async-pipeline concurrency bug itself remains open and would need a dedicated investigation if high-concurrency loadAsync usage is needed elsewhere.
    6.19.0 Two more Scene Editor 3D reload fixes (editor/scene_editor3d.lua), found immediately after 6.18.0 when the user retested and every placed mesh (still correctly counted in "Placed Meshes") rendered stacked on top of a single one at the map's center. Root cause: writeScene3d/tPlacedMeshInfo only ever stores each mesh's final, flattened WORLD position -- it discards grid-cell indices and map type entirely at save time (resolvePlacedMeshWorldPos already collapsed everything down before writing). onOpenScene3d's reload correctly sets each entry's freeX/freeZ from that saved world position, but resolvePlacedMeshWorldPos only actually reads freeX/freeZ when tMapOptions.sMapType == 'Free' -- for any scene authored in the (non-Free-only) grid modes, e.g. via "Fill Layer with selected mesh" which explicitly requires non-Free mode, the reload left cellX/cellZ at their default 0/0 and resolvePlacedMeshWorldPos took the grid-cell branch instead, so every single reloaded mesh resolved to the exact same cell (0,0). Fixed by forcing tMapOptions.sMapType = 'Free' before the reload loop, since flattened world positions are all the save format has ever kept -- Free mode's positioning is the only one that can correctly reconstruct them. Also fixed getHeader3d's generated comment, which described "require this file and call :load()" usage (correct for an exported game scene) even when writing the editor's own .scene3d-edit.lua save file, which is reopened via the editor's own Load, never required by a game -- writeScene3d/getHeader3d now take a bIsExport flag and emit distinct, accurate header text for the two cases. Both fixes verified headlessly: a mock 5-mesh reload starting from non-Free map mode now resolves to 5 correct, distinct world positions instead of one shared spot, and the save-file header no longer mentions requiring the file while the exported-file header still does.
    6.20.0 Scene Editor 3D Save now round-trips full editor authoring state instead of just flattened mesh world-positions (editor/scene_editor3d.lua), fixing a direct user report: rebuilding a 400-mesh scene with all-default settings (Orthogonal map, 100x100 grid cells, 20x20 count) and saving showed none of those settings anywhere in the file, and reloading it silently switched the map to Free mode (the prior 6.19.0 fix, needed only because the format had nothing better to restore from), collapsed all layers into one synthetic default, and left the Mesh Set/Mesh Selector panels empty. writeScene3d now writes tScene3d.tMapOptions and tScene3d.tLayers (via the same tUtil.save-backed writeSavedTable helper already used for tLightConfig), a wholesale tScene3d.tOptionsEditor (resolution settings, last-run script, initial camera), and extends each tPlacedMeshInfo entry with layerIndex/cellX/cellZ/freeX/freeZ/freeY/bAttachedToLayer alongside the existing flattened x/y/z -- all of this gated behind "not bIsExport" so Export Game Scene's output stays exactly as clean as before (verified: grepped the exported file for zero occurrences of tMapOptions/tLayers/cellX/bAttachedToLayer). onOpenScene3d's restore logic was extracted into a standalone applyLoadedScene3d(tLoaded) function (directly unit-testable without a native file dialog) that now: restores tMapOptions outright; rebuilds tLayers from scratch in saved order before touching any placed mesh, since layerIndex is a positional index into tLayers that's meaningless until the array exists in the same shape it was saved in; removes the 6.19.0 sMapType='Free' force-hack entirely, since real map-type + per-mesh cell/free data now round-trips correctly under whatever mode the scene actually used; calls registerMeshSetEntry for every placed mesh's fileName so the Mesh Set tab and the separate Mesh Selector window (same underlying tMeshSetEntries data) show the scene's actual meshes regardless of whether the original asset folder is even reachable; and restores the initial camera position/focus (tOptionsEditor.fSceneCamPos/fSceneCamFocus -- written since the Export feature was added, silently never read back until now). Also fixed a latent, independent transform bug this rewrite exposed: addPlacedMesh's synchronous path already positions the loaded object using its own hardcoded defaults (bAttachedToLayer=true, freeY=0, rotationY=0, scale={1,1,1}) before returning, so the reload loop's post-hoc patches to those same fields on the returned record were updating the data only, never the live object's actual rendered transform -- fixed by calling syncPlacedMeshTransform once more after the patches. Every new field is read with a fallback matching the old format's exact prior behavior, so files saved before this change (e.g. the real fixture this bug was found from) still load correctly with no error. Verified headlessly end-to-end: a mock scene with a custom grid, two distinct layers (grid-cell and free-position meshes on different layers), and lights round-trips every value exactly (map options, layer order/fY/offset, per-mesh resolved world position, rotation, scale, attachment mode, camera, Mesh Set entries), the corresponding Export output contains none of the new editor-only fields, and the real pre-existing old-format 400-mesh save file still loads without error.
    6.21.0 Four more Scene Editor 3D fixes (editor/scene_editor3d.lua), found from continued testing of 6.20.0's full save/load round-trip. (1) The editor's own per-tab orbit-navigation viewport camera (tCamByTab: azimuth/elevation/distance/focus for "Main Scene" and "Map edition") was never saved or restored -- only tOptionsEditor.fSceneCamPos/fSceneCamFocus (the exported game's initial camera, a completely separate system) was. Now writeScene3d writes tScene3d.tCamByTab (Save-only, same writeSavedTable mechanism) and applyLoadedScene3d restores it wholesale; cam3d needs no re-aliasing since main_tab_bar() already re-derives it from tCamByTab every frame. (2) Lights loaded as "off" even when tLightConfig.bEnabled was true and correctly restored, until some unrelated light edit (e.g. adding a point light) incidentally pushed the whole config to the engine as a side effect -- applyLightConfigToEngine() was only ever called from UI actions, never automatically after a reload restored tLightConfig's data. Fixed by calling it explicitly at the end of that restore step. (3) A scene-object marker's name typed into its InputText field never stuck. Root cause: tImGui.InputText(label, text, flags?) has no "max length" parameter at all (its C++ binding always auto-sizes the edit buffer from the current text's own length); the "64" passed at this call site expecting some other framework's (label, text, maxLength) shape was instead validated as a raw ImGuiInputTextFlags bitmask, and 64 == ImGuiInputTextFlags_EnterReturnsTrue -- a real, valid flag -- silently changing the field's contract to "only report a change once Enter is pressed" instead of on every keystroke. Removed the erroneous argument. Also fixed every other widget in the same function (drawObjectMarkerFields), which used bare, non-marker-specific ImGui IDs ('##marker_x', '##marker_w', etc, and the nested points loop only disambiguated by point index, not marker index) -- with two or more markers open at once, they all shared one underlying widget/edit-buffer per field. (4) Switching the Map tab's type between Orthogonal/Isometric and Free reset every placed mesh's visible position. Root cause: resolvePlacedMeshWorldPos only ever reads {cellX,cellZ} or {freeX,freeZ,freeY}, whichever the current map type selects -- the other pair is stale/never populated for a given mesh (e.g. a grid-placed mesh's freeX/freeZ are left at addPlacedMesh's default of 0), so switching modes with no resync collapsed every such mesh onto the origin. Fixed with a capture-before/apply-after pair (captureAllPlacedMeshWorldPositions/applyPlacedMeshWorldPositions) around the mode change in drawMapTab's combo handler -- position is snapshotted while the old mode/rotation is still active, then re-derived into both field pairs once the new mode is in effect, so grid cell indices are computed with the correct (new) rotation. (1) and (2) were user-verified fixed directly; (4) was verified headlessly (round-trip position-preservation check both switch directions) -- the first version of that fix called the new capture/apply split in the wrong order relative to the mode change, which the test correctly caught as a failure before the call order was corrected and the test re-run clean.
    6.22.0 Three more Scene Editor 3D features/fixes (editor/scene_editor3d.lua). (1) Main Scene tab object markers (point/rectangle/circle/triangle/line) now render a real shape at their actual position/size instead of a single always-cyan point stub -- point is a cube, circle is a UV-sphere (both raw-vertex unit geometry, scaled per-instance via :setScale() since no named 'sphere'/'cube' primitive exists in SHAPE_MESH's Lua binding), rectangle/triangle are flat quads via the existing named-primitive path, and line now renders the actual connected path through tObj.points instead of a single dangling point. All five colored semi-transparent magenta ({1,0,1,0.7}), matching Tile Map Editor's own "this is an editor marker, not real geometry" convention. updateSceneObjectShapes() now recreates a marker's shape whenever its type (or, for line, its point count) changes, which the old version never did. (2) Added WASD/arrow-key ground-plane movement for the viewport's orbit-navigation camera (cam3d), consumed once per frame gated on tImGui.GetWantCaptureKeyboard() so typing in an ImGui field doesn't also pan the camera -- reuses the same forward/right XZ-plane derivation already used by right-drag-pan, so keyboard and mouse panning feel consistent. (3) Fixed Run/Play doing nothing (a blank window on Play): onPlay3d's temp file is a require-style module (ends in `return tScene3d`), which defines no onInitScene/onLoop of its own, so a freshly spawned engine instance loading it directly as --scene renders nothing. scene_editor2d.lua already solved this with a createBasicScriptForScene() helper that generates a small separate driver script and a "create it for me" Run-menu button -- scene_editor3d.lua had the sCurrentScriptExecution field and the manual file-picker button but neither the generator nor the button, so Run could never work unless a driver was hand-written first. Added the matching createBasicScriptForScene3d() (also includes a WASD fly-camera in the generated driver, operating directly on mbm.getCamera('3d') since the exported game has no cam3d/orbit-state) and the "create it for me"/clear-script buttons in menu_run, mirroring 2D's exact structure. All three verified headlessly (shape/type creation and rebuild-on-change, simulated key down/up driving cam3d.fx/fz over several frames, and generated-driver syntax/content checks with mbm.saveFile stubbed out so the test never blocks on a native dialog).
    6.23.0 Seven follow-up adjustments to 6.22.0's Scene Editor 3D work (editor/scene_editor3d.lua), from direct user testing. (1) Object marker fields (drawObjectMarkerFields) switched from typed InputFloat to DragFloat, using the same scene-range convention already established for point lights (getPointLightDragRange) -- typed coordinates had no sense of the scene's actual scale, dragging within the grid's real extent is the same "grab and move it" interaction the point-light panel already had. Rectangle/circle's width/height/ray gained the same treatment. Triangle markers are now genuinely 3-point shapes (tObj.points, each independently draggable, default-initialized around the marker's position on first becoming a triangle) instead of a fixed 100x100 stand-in, with tObj.x/y/z now a "move the whole shape" control that translates all 3 points by the same delta when dragged. Line markers' existing per-point fields got the same DragFloat treatment, and "add point" now continues the previous segment's exact direction AND length (new = last + (last - secondLast), simple linear extrapolation) instead of always defaulting to the marker's own current position -- mirroring, but more precise than, Tile Map Editor's own line tool (which only steps by a fixed one-tile-size regardless of the actual previous segment length). (2)/(3) Object markers were staying visible on every tab, not just Main Scene/Map -- updateSceneObjectShapes()'s `.visible = (sActiveTab=='map')` toggle only ever ran from inside drawMapTab's own UI block, so it never got a chance to flip markers back off once the user switched to a different tab. Now also called unconditionally from onLoop every frame. Added a bShowSceneObjectMarkers checkbox (ANDed into the same visibility expression) so markers can be hidden manually regardless of tab. (4) Rectangle markers were only visible from one side -- the named 'rectangle' SHAPE_MESH primitive is a single-sided, backface-culled quad. Rewritten as a raw-vertex unit quad with two coplanar triangle sets (front winding + reversed-winding back), so one side is always front-facing regardless of view direction; triangle markers (built from their own 3 points, not axis-aligned) got the same double-sided treatment for free since they're built the same way, rebuilt (destroy/recreate, no in-place update exists for SHAPE_MESH) whenever a signature of their 3 points changes. Line markers switched from destroy/recreate to LINE_MESH:set() for in-place point updates (confirmed via line-mesh-lua.cpp's onSetLineMeshLua to have no size constraint vs the previous point count) -- needed once points became live-draggable, since the old count-only rebuild trigger left a stale rendered path while an existing point's coordinates were being dragged. (5) Play with "Load meshes asynchronously" enabled crashed the spawned second instance. Root cause: this is the same confirmed engine-level bug already on file (async-mesh-load-concurrency-crash memory/commit history) -- mesh:loadAsync segfaults under many concurrent requests for the SAME file, and a level with repeated placements of one prop fires exactly that pattern when the generated tScene3d.load's plain for-loop calls loadAsync once per instance with no throttling at all. The underlying C++ pipeline bug remains open (shared infrastructure, needs dedicated investigation), but the async-mode generated loader now routes every mesh load through a new _loadMeshAsyncQueued method: a per-fileName queue that only ever allows 1 loadAsync in flight for a given file, dispatching the next queued request only once the previous one's callback fires, while different files still load fully in parallel (only repeated instances of the same file are serialized) -- omitted entirely from sync-mode's generated code (no dead code). (6)/(7) WASD's A/D strafe moved the camera opposite to what "look toward -Z, A goes left" actually means. Root cause: both the editor's own updateCam3dKeyboardMovement and the generated Play driver's onLoop re-derived a "right" vector by hand as cross(forward, worldUp) = (-fwz, fwx) -- the wrong handedness for this engine's actual coordinate convention. Replaced with camera:getNormal('R')/('F') (src/lua-wrap/camera-lua.cpp), which read the engine's own true normalRight/normalForward directly instead of guessing, in both places (the editor's viewport camera and the generated driver template, which had independently duplicated the same bug). All seven verified headlessly: marker field editing exercised through the real drawObjectMarkerFields function inside an open ImGui frame (no screenshots, per explicit preference), triangle center-drag/point-rebuild and line in-place-update behavior confirmed via handle-identity/signature checks, the "add point" continuation formula checked directly, marker visibility checked across tab switches and the new checkbox, WASD's D-key movement direction checked via dot product against camera:getNormal('R') itself, and the async queue's core same-file-serialization property confirmed with a pure-Lua functional test simulating 15 concurrent same-file requests plus 5 different-file requests (max concurrency observed: exactly 1 per file, all 20 requests still eventually complete).
    6.23.1 Fixed a real bug in 6.23.0's own triangle-marker rebuild (editor/scene_editor3d.lua, updateSceneObjectShapes), found immediately by the user testing it: dragging a triangle marker's position or any of its 3 points visibly did nothing, and separate triangle markers all appeared to render as the exact same shape/size. Root cause: every rebuild called shape:create(verts, nil, 'editor_marker_triangle') with the SAME fixed literal nickname, but SHAPE_MESH::load (src/render/shape-mesh.cpp:859) resolves that nickname through MESH_MANAGER::load -- a cache keyed by name, SHARED across every SHAPE_MESH object in the process, not a per-instance one-time-reload guard as assumed. The first triangle ever created under that name got its geometry cached under 'editor_marker_triangle'; every later :create() call using that same literal name -- for that same marker's own subsequent edits, or for any OTHER triangle marker entirely -- silently got back that first triangle's stale cached mesh instead of the new vertex data just passed in. Fixed by giving each rebuild a genuinely unique nickname (marker index + a per-marker running rebuild counter, 'editor_marker_triangle_<i>_<count>'), so a rebuild's new points always load fresh geometry instead of hitting someone else's cache entry; applied the same defensive uniqueness to the transient placeholder nickname created on first switching a marker to 'triangle'. Verified this time by actually inspecting rendered geometry, not just handle identity (the previous round's test would have passed even with this bug, since a fresh shape object is still created each rebuild regardless of nickname collision -- only the geometry it ends up displaying was wrong): two differently-sized triangle markers now report genuinely different :getSize() bounds (20x20 vs 1000x1000), and editing an existing triangle's points changes its own reported size accordingly (confirmed going from 20x20 to 1000x1000 after an edit, where before this fix it would have stayed stuck at whichever triangle was created first).
    6.24.0 Fixed the root cause of a Scene Editor 3D bug reported by the user: large static meshes baked flat-white Mesh Set/Mesh Selector thumbnails in the Map edition tab. render2texture's own internal 3D/2D cameras (CAMERA_TARGET, render-2-texture.cpp) have always had real, independent near/far clip planes (zNear/zFar for 3D, zNear2d/zFar2d for 2D, defaulting to 0.1/1000 and -100/100) with working C++ getters/setters, but render-2-texture-lua.cpp's rt:getCamera(...) never exposed them to Lua at all (only pos/focus/scale/angle/up/move were bound) -- unlike the main scene camera (mbm.getCamera("3d")), which has always had cam:setNear/cam:setFar. scene_editor3d.lua's processThumbnailQueue frames each mesh by placing that camera at a distance proportional to the mesh's own bounding box (fitDist); for any mesh large enough to push that distance past the hardcoded 1000-unit far plane, the object landed entirely outside the frustum and render2texture's capture pass drew nothing, leaving the target at its clear color (white). Added the missing bindings: onSetNear/onGetNear/onSetFar/onGetFar (three.js/mbm.getCamera-style, driving zNear/zFar) plus 2D counterparts onSetNear2d/onGetNear2d/onSetFar2d/onGetFar2d (driving zNear2d/zFar2d) in render-2-texture-lua.cpp, registered under the same Lua names ("setNear"/"getNear"/"setFar"/"getFar") on both rt:getCamera("3d") and rt:getCamera("2d"), dispatched by the existing is3d flag so each mode's Lua calls always touch the field that actually affects its own projection. processThumbnailQueue now calls rtCam:setNear/setFar scaled to each mesh's own fitDist right before framing it, replacing an earlier same-session workaround (temporarily normalizing the preview object's scale so its footprint stayed inside the fixed 0.1..1000 range) that worked but didn't address why the binding was missing.
    6.25.0 Finished the async mesh-loading worker-thread path-resolution fix that docs/async-mesh-path-resolution-refactor.md had left unimplemented, and found a real gap in that plan's own scope while doing so: parse_v11_intermediate() (mesh-manager.cpp) is called from both MESH_MANAGER::Impl::workerLoop() (the async/worker-thread path the doc analyzed) AND the synchronous MESH_MBM::loadV11() (reached via MESH_MANAGER::load(), main-thread, called with the caller's raw/relative fileName) -- the doc's claim that synchronous loading was untouched conflated MESH_MBM::loadV11() with the genuinely-separate MESH_MBM_DEBUG::loadV11(). Applying the doc's proposed fix (parse_v11_intermediate stops calling util::openFile/getFullPath, a call with an addPath side effect not safe off the main thread) without also fixing the sync call site would have silently broken MESH_MANAGER::load() for any mesh referenced by a relative path. Fixed by resolving the path via util::getFullPath() up front, on the main thread, in both MESH_MANAGER::loadAsync() and MESH_MBM::loadV11(), while keeping the original caller-supplied fileName flowing unchanged into finishLoadFromIntermediate/impl->fileName in both paths (needed since onLoadMeshLua, mesh-lua.cpp, does a raw string compare against getFileName() to skip redundant reloads) -- only the path used to actually open the file is the resolved one. Exposed the previously file-local util::fopenApp (file-util.cpp) as a public util-interface.h/file-util.h primitive -- opens an already-resolved path directly with zero getFullPath/addPath side effects, safe from any thread -- which parse_v11_intermediate now uses instead of util::openFile, so mesh loading's worker threads no longer touch the general addPath mutex/deferred-queue safety net at all (which itself remains in place for any other worker-thread caller). Also made MESH_MANAGER::Impl::ensureWorkersStarted's worker count dynamic -- was a hardcoded constexpr 2, now computed from std::thread::hardware_concurrency(), clamped to [2, 8]. A third change from the same pass -- making MESH_MANAGER::loadAsync's cache-hit branch fire onComplete inline instead of round-tripping through pumpAsyncLoads' completedJobs queue -- was tried, then reverted after it crashed a real Scene Editor 3D "Run" scene with 400 placed instances of one mesh: editor/scene_editor3d.lua's generated _loadMeshAsyncQueued chains same-file loadAsync requests by calling processNext() again from inside its own callback, relying on the old "callback never fires before this call returns" contract; once cache hits fired inline, every request after the first for one file resolved synchronously and recursed with no yield back to the event loop, exhausting the C/Lua call stack (reproduced as SIGABRT). MESH_MANAGER::loadAsync's cache-hit handling, Impl::AsyncResult's cacheHit/cachedMesh fields, and the "never inline, even on a cache hit" contract documented on both MESH_MANAGER::loadAsync and MESH::loadAsync are back exactly as before -- see docs/async-mesh-path-resolution-refactor.md's "Tried and reverted" section for the full writeup and the lesson for next time this is reconsidered (checking call-site *state ordering* isn't enough; a bounded-but-large-N recursive continuation pattern only breaks at real scale).
*/
#define MBM_VERSION "6.25.0" // MBM_VERSION must be in format X.Y or X.Y.Z
#endif

#endif
